## Why This Is Asked
Tests constant-time, multi-word popcount without branches. Bridges knowledge of SWAR patterns from 64-bit into 128-bit by handling halves separately. It also checks handling of non-power-of-two widths.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR/popcount on 64-bit
- 128-bit word handling (hi/lo)
- Branch-free, mask-shift arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t countSetBits64(uint64_t v){
  v = v - ((v>>1)&0x5555555555555555ULL);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((v>>2)&0x3333333333333333ULL);
  v = (v + (v>>4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint32_t countSetBits128(uint128 x){
  return countSetBits64(x.lo) + countSetBits64(x.hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values?
- How to verify edge cases (all-ones, zero) across both halves?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform cross-128-bit bit counting without loops, using portable bit-twiddling. Demonstrates knowledge of XOR for difference, and multi-word popcount without hardware intrinsics.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based difference
- SWAR-style popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining counts for 128-bit data

## Code Example

```c
unsigned distance128(uint128 a, uint128 b) {
  uint64_t x = a.hi ^ b.hi;
  uint64_t y = a.lo ^ b.lo;
  // SWAR popcount on x
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  // SWAR popcount on y
  y = y - ((y >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  y = (y & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((y >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  y = (y + (y >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = y + (y >> 8);
  y = y + (y >> 16);
  y = y + (y >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0xFF) + (unsigned)(y & 0xFF);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to arbitrary n-word widths while maintaining no-loops constraints?
- Could you optimize using compiler intrinsics if available and fall back gracefully? If so, which intrinsics and how would you keep portability?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests precise bit permutation across 128-bit boundary without loops. Requires careful cross-half carry handling when shifting hi/lo.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value as {hi, lo}
- Masks for even/odd bit positions across both halves
- Cross-half carry during shifts between lo and hi

## Code Example

```javascript
// Outline (not full implementation)
uint128 swapAdjacentBits128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 evenMask = {0x5555555555555555ULL, 0x5555555555555555ULL};
  uint128 oddMask  = {0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL, 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAULL};
  uint64_t lo_e = x.lo & evenMask.lo; uint64_t hi_e = x.hi & evenMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_o = x.lo & oddMask.lo;  uint64_t hi_o = x.hi & oddMask.hi;
  uint64_t lo_left  = (lo_e << 1);
  uint64_t hi_left  = (hi_e << 1) | (lo_e >> 63);
  uint64_t lo_right = (lo_o >> 1);
  uint64_t hi_right = (hi_o >> 1);
  uint128 res;
  res.lo = lo_left | lo_right;
  res.hi = hi_left | hi_right;
  // handle cross-half carry from lo_o/hi_o if needed
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to arbitrary bit widths or detect overflow across the 128-bit boundary?
- How would you unit-test edge cases: all zeros, all ones, alternating patterns, and patterns crossing the boundary?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bitwise folding to compute parity without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parity via XOR folding
- Bitwise shifts to mix bits
- No branches or loops in parity path

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t parity32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t p = n;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

